    "case_summary": "An 82-year-old man with a 60 pack-year history of tobacco use presented with progressive dysphagia, first to solids and then to liquids, over a three-month period. He experienced episodes of food getting stuck in his throat, weight loss of more than 100 lbs, night sweats, and hoarseness. A productive cough was noted, but there was no fever, chest pain, or hemoptysis.\n\nPhysical findings included:- Cachexia and exaggerated cervical lordosis- Normal lymph nodes- Tongue paralysis with atrophy on cranial nerve testing\n\n1. Computed Tomography (CT) of neck:- Atrophy of the tongue- Large confluent anterior cervical osteophytes from C2–T1- Bilateral noncalcified pulmonary nodules at lung apices- Enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes2. CT of chest:- Diffuse innumerable pulmonary nodules predominantly in lung bases- Small mediastinal and hilar nodes3. Speech-Language Pathology Evaluation:- Modified barium swallow study showed:  * No epiglottic inversion  * Reduced laryngeal and hyoid excursion  * Reduced arytenoid movement  * Deep anterior wall aspiration  * Weak, delayed cough reflex4. Brain MRI:- Showed chronic ischemic changes, no acute abnormalities5. Cervical spine MRI:- Revealed multilevel bulky large anterior osteophytes6. Flexible laryngoscopy:- Large retropharyngeal mass partially obstructing airway- Mobile vocal cords bilaterally7. Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD):- Extrinsic compression of upper esophagusImage Information:- Figure 1: CT scan showing large confluent anterior cervical osteophytes- Figure 2: MRI showing multilevel degenerative changes with bulky large anterior osteophytes- Figure 3: EGD showing bulging in upper esophagus with significant compression",
